    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Circadian modulation of contextual and cued fear conditioning (FC) has been investigated in mice, but not in the Siberian hamster, Phodopus sungorus.  This study tested whether measuring locomotor activity suppression, a validated index of fear in rodents, during FC reveals a circadian rhythm to FC performance, both in hippocampal and non-hippocampal dependent memory tasks.  Animals were exposed to a series of tones followed by footshocks during the first two days of training, then tested for their cued recollection of the tone in a novel environment two days later.  Locomotion and activity state were continuously measured via video during testing.  Our data suggest that both contextual and cued memory ability are stronger during the light phase than the dark, as indicated by a greater increase in inactivity measurements during the light.  Therefore, there exists a significant circadian rhythm in P. sungorus contextual and cued FC, although the latter’s rhythm only manifests itself after several exposures to the tone.  On the whole, there were few appreciable sex differences, with the exception of the Inactive Events and Bouts measurements for contextual FC.  These results verify the robustness of FC and the inactivity measurement as an evaluation of learning and memory in P. sungorus.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
